---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Kashyapa Maharishi — The Sacred Legacy | Sree Pulikkal Vishwanagayakshi Temple">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="km-hero">
    <div class="km-hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="km-hero-content">
      <span class="hero-eyebrow">The Living Root of This Sacred Land</span>
      <h1>Kashyapa Maharishi</h1>
      <p>Saptarishi. Father of the Nagas. The ancient sage whose divine presence consecrates every grain of this sacred earth.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Opening Verse -->
  <section class="verse-section">
    <div class="verse-container">
      <blockquote class="opening-verse">
        "To step onto this land is not to visit a temple — it is to enter the living ashram of Sage Kashyapa, whose prayers still echo through these sacred groves."
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: The Sage -->
  <section class="story-section bg-base">
    <div class="story-container">
      <div class="story-label">The Saptarishi</div>
      <h2>Who Was Kashyapa Maharishi?</h2>

      <div class="story-grid">
        <div class="story-text">
          <p class="story-lead">
            Among the seven great sages — the Saptarishis — who form the pillars of Vedic civilization,
            Kashyapa Maharishi stands as one of the most foundational. He is not simply a historical figure
            of ancient scripture; he is a cosmic architect whose lineage gave rise to the very fabric of creation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sage Kashyapa is revered as the progenitor of the Devas, the Asuras, and — most significantly
            for this sacred land — the Nagas. Through his divine union with Kadru, the great serpent race
            was born. Every Naga deity worshipped in the world traces its sacred lineage to this great sage.
          </p>
          <p>
            His wisdom was so profound that it birthed its own scripture — the <em>Kashyapa Samhita</em> —
            a revered Vedic text that governs the worship of the Nagas to this day. At the Pulikkal
            Vishwanagayakshi Temple, the rituals performed by Ambotti Thampuran draw directly from
            this ancient tradition established by the sage himself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="story-facts">
          <div class="fact-card">
            <div class="fact-icon">📿</div>
            <h4>Saptarishi</h4>
            <p>One of the seven cosmic sages who embody the divine wisdom of the Vedas</p>
          </div>
          <div class="fact-card">
            <div class="fact-icon">🐍</div>
            <h4>Father of the Nagas</h4>
            <p>Through Kadru, father of the entire Naga race — the divine serpent deities</p>
          </div>
          <div class="fact-card">
            <div class="fact-icon">📖</div>
            <h4>Kashyapa Samhita</h4>
            <p>Author of the sacred text governing authentic Naga worship traditions</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: The Ashram -->
  <section class="story-section bg-alt">
    <div class="story-container">
      <div class="story-label">The Ancient Hermitage</div>
      <h2>The Kashyapa Ashram</h2>

      <div class="story-text-wide">
        <p class="story-lead">
          The land of Kavilpad in Palakkad, Kerala — where the Pulikkal Shankarodath Kovilakam
          stands today — was once the vibrant hermitage of Sage Kashyapa himself.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was on this very earth that the great Maharishi performed his most intense tapas
          (sacred austerities), invoked the divine, and established a space of extraordinary
          cosmic energy. The ancient lore of the temple affirms that the sage chose this land
          not by chance, but through divine sankalpa — a conscious spiritual intention that
          permanently consecrated the ground beneath.
        </p>
        <p>
          The primary deities enshrined in the temple's Srilakam — the sanctum sanctorum —
          are believed to be rooted in the <em>Thevara Sankalpa</em>: the original divine
          invocation and worship rituals first initiated by Sage Kashyapa on this soil.
          What the priests perform today is not ceremony — it is a continuation of that
          primordial act of worship.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the devotee who understands this, walking into the temple complex is not
          an ordinary act of faith. It is an act of stepping into a living tradition,
          breathing the same consecrated air that once surrounded a sage of cosmic stature.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: The Nilavara -->
  <section class="nilavara-section">
    <div class="nilavara-container">
      <div class="nilavara-content">
        <div class="story-label light">The Sacred Heart</div>
        <h2>The Nilavara — Eternal Abode of the Sage</h2>
        <p>
          Within the sacred precincts of the traditional Nalambalam — the inner temple enclosure —
          lies the ancestral Nilavara: a consecrated underground vault of profound spiritual
          significance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Temple lore and the unbroken memory of its lineage hold that this vault enshrines
          the eternal spiritual presence of Kashyapa Maharishi — his Samadhi, where his divine
          consciousness remains permanently established. This is not legend alone; it is the
          living conviction of every priest and devotee who has felt the extraordinary energy
          that radiates from within.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside this deeply consecrated space, Kashyapa Maharishi is worshipped with profound
          reverence as <strong>Nagamuthassan</strong> — the Grandfather of all Nagas. The
          Chaitanya, the divine consciousness of the sage and the devathas he invoked, are
          believed to reside here permanently, silently blessing all who seek with a pure heart.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is this eternal presence that imbues the temple with an energy unlike any other
          Naga kshetra in Kerala. Devotees who pray here do not merely offer rituals to an
          enshrined deity — they pray in the very presence of the sage who gave birth to the
          Nagas, on the very earth he consecrated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: The Naga Trinity -->
  <section class="story-section bg-base">
    <div class="story-container">
      <div class="story-label">The Living Legacy</div>
      <h2>The Divine Naga Trinity</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Because this land is the ashram of the father of the Nagas, the serpent worship here
        carries a potency that is rare in the spiritual world. Three supreme Naga deities are
        worshipped together in a single sanctum — a distinction unique to this temple.
      </p>

      <div class="trinity-grid">
        <div class="trinity-card">
          <div class="trinity-number">I</div>
          <h3>Nagaraja Swamy</h3>
          <p>
            The King of Serpents — the direct divine offspring of Sage Kashyapa and Kadru.
            His presence here, on his father's sacred land, is the homecoming of a divine
            son to his father's abode.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="trinity-card featured">
          <div class="trinity-number">II</div>
          <h3>Vishwanagayakshi Amma</h3>
          <p>
            The reigning Empress of the Yaksha Kula and self-manifested (Swayambhu)
            matriarch of the Nagas. Her divine manifestation on this land is inseparable
            from the sanctity established by the sage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="trinity-card">
          <div class="trinity-number">III</div>
          <h3>Sarpayakshi Amma</h3>
          <p>
            The sovereign mother and eternal protector of all serpents. Her presence
            completes the divine trinity, creating a confluence of Naga energy found
            nowhere else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Why It Matters -->
  <section class="story-section bg-alt">
    <div class="story-container">
      <div class="story-label">For the Devotee</div>
      <h2>Why This Sacred Land Is Extraordinary</h2>

      <div class="significance-grid">
        <div class="significance-card">
          <div class="sig-icon">🌿</div>
          <h3>Unbroken Vedic Lineage</h3>
          <p>
            Guided by Ambotti Thampuran, the temple preserves a form of worship that
            draws directly from the Kashyapa Samhita — the same sacred text authored
            by the sage whose presence graces this land. This is Vedic tradition in
            its most authentic form.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="significance-card">
          <div class="sig-icon">✨</div>
          <h3>Amplified Grace</h3>
          <p>
            Praying in the presence of a Maharishi's eternal abode is considered among
            the most auspicious acts in the Hindu tradition. Devotees seeking relief from
            Naga Dosha, Kala Sarpa Dosha, or life's persistent obstacles find the
            remedies performed here carry an exceptional depth of efficacy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="significance-card">
          <div class="sig-icon">🌳</div>
          <h3>The Seven Sacred Kavus</h3>
          <p>
            Surrounding the sage's eternal abode are seven highly protected Sarpa Kavus —
            sacred groves that serve as the ecological and spiritual guardians of his
            consecrated land. Devotees who walk through these untamed groves report
            experiencing a profound quieting of the mind and a deep physical peace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="significance-card">
          <div class="sig-icon">🔱</div>
          <h3>The Father's Own Land</h3>
          <p>
            Nowhere else does one worship Nagaraja on the very soil where his father,
            the sage who gave birth to the entire Naga race, established his ashram.
            The prayers offered here carry a filial energy — a son receiving blessings
            in his father's own home.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing CTA -->
  <section class="km-cta-section">
    <div class="km-cta-container">
      <h2>Come. This Land Awaits You.</h2>
      <p>
        For centuries, seekers have arrived at this sacred ground carrying the weight of
        unseen obstacles, ancestral burdens, and unanswered prayers. And they have left
        — lighter, quieter, and touched by a grace that belongs to this ancient earth alone.
      </p>
      <div class="km-cta-buttons">
        <a href="/poojas" class="btn-km-primary">Explore Sacred Rituals</a>
        <a href="/visit" class="btn-km-secondary">Plan Your Visit</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>

<style>
/* Hero */
.km-hero {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 80vh;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  background:
    linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.55), rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.92)),
    url('/images/temple1.jpg') center/cover;
  padding: 8rem 2rem 5rem;
  text-align: center;
}

.km-hero-overlay {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: radial-gradient(circle at 50% 60%, rgba(212, 165, 116, 0.12) 0%, transparent 65%);
}

.km-hero-content {
  max-width: 850px;
  position: relative;
  z-index: 10;
}

.hero-eyebrow {
  display: inline-block;
  font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: var(--gold-light);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 5px;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}

.km-hero h1 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 5.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: white;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  line-height: 1.05;
  text-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
}

.km-hero p {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  color: var(--gold-light);
  line-height: 1.7;
  font-style: italic;
  max-width: 720px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

/* Verse */
.verse-section {
  background: var(--laterite-dark);
  padding: 4rem 2rem;
  border-bottom: 3px solid var(--gold-light);
}

.verse-container {
  max-width: 900px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  text-align: center;
}

.opening-verse {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 2rem;
  font-style: italic;
  color: var(--gold-light);
  line-height: 1.7;
  margin: 0;
}

/* Story Sections */
.story-section {
  padding: 6rem 2rem;
}

.bg-base { background: var(--bg-base); }
.bg-alt  { background: var(--bg-alt); }

.story-container {
  max-width: 1100px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.story-label {
  font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  color: var(--gold);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 4px;
  font-weight: 600;
  margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
}

.story-label.light {
  color: var(--gold-light);
}

.story-container h2 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 3.2rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  margin-bottom: 3rem;
}

.story-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr;
  gap: 4rem;
  align-items: start;
}

.story-lead {
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  line-height: 1.9;
  color: var(--laterite-dark);
  font-weight: 500;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}

.story-text p,
.story-text-wide p {
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  line-height: 1.9;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}

.story-text p em,
.story-text-wide p em {
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  font-style: italic;
}

.story-text-wide {
  max-width: 820px;
}

.section-intro {
  font-size: 1.2rem;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  line-height: 1.8;
  margin-bottom: 3rem;
  max-width: 780px;
}

/* Fact Cards */
.story-facts {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1.5rem;
  position: sticky;
  top: 5rem;
}

.fact-card {
  background: var(--bg-alt);
  border-radius: 12px;
  padding: 1.8rem;
  border-left: 4px solid var(--laterite-red);
  text-align: center;
}

.fact-icon {
  font-size: 2.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
}

.fact-card h4 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}

.fact-card p {
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* Nilavara Section */
.nilavara-section {
  background:
    linear-gradient(rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.93), rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.93)),
    url('/images/temple.jpg') center/cover fixed;
  padding: 7rem 2rem;
}

.nilavara-container {
  max-width: 850px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.nilavara-content h2 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 3.2rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--gold-light);
  margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
}

.nilavara-content p {
  font-size: 1.15rem;
  line-height: 1.95;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
}

.nilavara-content strong {
  color: var(--gold-light);
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* Trinity */
.trinity-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 2rem;
}

.trinity-card {
  background: var(--bg-alt);
  padding: 2.5rem 2rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
  border-top: 4px solid var(--border-light);
  text-align: center;
  transition: transform 0.3s ease, box-shadow 0.3s ease;
}

.trinity-card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-6px);
  box-shadow: 0 15px 40px rgba(122, 25, 18, 0.12);
}

.trinity-card.featured {
  border-top-color: var(--laterite-red);
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff9e6 0%, var(--bg-alt) 100%);
}

.trinity-number {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 3.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--border-light);
  line-height: 1;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

.trinity-card.featured .trinity-number {
  color: var(--saffron);
}

.trinity-card h3 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

.trinity-card p {
  font-size: 1rem;
  line-height: 1.8;
  color: var(--text-muted);
}

/* Significance Grid */
.significance-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: 2.5rem;
}

.significance-card {
  background: var(--bg-base);
  padding: 2.5rem;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 6px 25px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.07);
}

.sig-icon {
  font-size: 3rem;
  margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
}

.significance-card h3 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 1.7rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
}

.significance-card p {
  font-size: 1.05rem;
  line-height: 1.85;
  color: var(--text-muted);
}

/* CTA */
.km-cta-section {
  padding: 7rem 2rem;
  background: var(--laterite-red);
  text-align: center;
  border-top: 6px solid var(--saffron);
}

.km-cta-container {
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.km-cta-section h2 {
  font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
  font-size: 3.8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: white;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}

.km-cta-section p {
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
  line-height: 1.9;
  margin-bottom: 3rem;
  font-style: italic;
}

.km-cta-buttons {
  display: flex;
  gap: 2rem;
  justify-content: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

.btn-km-primary,
.btn-km-secondary {
  padding: 1.2rem 3rem;
  font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 1.05rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1.5px;
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: all 0.3s ease;
}

.btn-km-primary {
  background: var(--gold);
  color: var(--text-dark);
  border: 2px solid var(--gold);
  box-shadow: 0 4px 0 rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.35);
}

.btn-km-primary:hover {
  background: var(--gold-light);
  transform: translateY(2px);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 0 rgba(45, 24, 16, 0.35);
}

.btn-km-secondary {
  background: transparent;
  color: white;
  border: 2px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
}

.btn-km-secondary:hover {
  background: white;
  color: var(--laterite-red);
  border-color: white;
}

/* Responsive */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .km-hero h1 { font-size: 3.5rem; }

  .story-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  .story-facts {
    position: static;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .fact-card {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 200px;
  }

  .trinity-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  .nilavara-content h2,
  .story-container h2 {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .km-hero h1 { font-size: 2.8rem; }
  .opening-verse { font-size: 1.4rem; }
  .km-cta-section h2 { font-size: 2.6rem; }
  .km-cta-buttons { flex-direction: column; align-items: center; }
  .story-facts { flex-direction: column; }
}
</style>
